---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The epidemic threshold — five small explainers"
  description="One slider showing how a disease flips from fizzling out to a full outbreak around a threshold contact rate."
  accent="amber"
  activePath={`${base}epidemic-threshold/`}
>
  <h1>Why one extra contact can flip fizzle into outbreak</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The same disease, spreading on the same network of people, with the same
    one starting case. The only thing the slider changes is how many people
    each infected person passes it to on average. Watch how little that
    number has to move before the outcome flips completely.
  </p>

  <section class="panel" aria-label="Simulation">
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="control">
        <label for="contact-slider"
          >Contact rate (≈ R₀) <output id="contact-output" for="contact-slider">1.0</output
          ></label
        >
        <input type="range" id="contact-slider" min="0.5" max="4" step="0.1" value="1.0" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="preset-caption">Try a real disease's ballpark R₀:</p>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-rate="1.3">Seasonal flu ≈1.3</button>
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-rate="2.5">COVID-19, original strain ≈2.5</button>
    </div>
    <p class="preset-note">
      Commonly cited ballpark estimates, simplified for illustration. Measles
      sits far higher still, around R₀ 12–18 — off this slider's range
      entirely.
    </p>

    <p class="preset-caption">Try an intervention, same disease:</p>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-rate="3.0">No precautions</button>
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-rate="1.8">Masks</button>
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-rate="1.0">Vaccination</button>
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-rate="0.6">Masks + distancing</button>
    </div>

    <p class="assumption">
      120 people, one starts infected, each stays infectious for 4 ticks
      before recovering. The network layout, patient zero, and the coin
      flips behind every run are fixed — only the two sliders below change
      anything: how many people each infected person passes it to, and how
      many people each person is even connected to in the first place.
    </p>

    <div class="controls">
      <div class="control">
        <label for="density-slider"
          >Average contacts per person <output id="density-output" for="density-slider"
            >≈5.6 contacts each</output
          ></label
        >
        <input type="range" id="density-slider" min="30" max="90" step="5" value="60" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <svg
      class="network"
      viewBox="0 0 600 400"
      role="img"
      aria-labelledby="network-title"
    >
      <title id="network-title">Network of people, coloured by infection status</title>
      <g id="network-edges"></g>
      <g id="network-nodes"></g>
    </svg>
    <p class="network-legend">
      <span class="swatch swatch-s"></span>Susceptible &nbsp;&nbsp;<span
        class="swatch swatch-i"
      ></span>Infected &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="swatch swatch-r"></span>Recovered
    </p>

    <svg class="growth-chart" viewBox="0 0 600 200" role="img" aria-labelledby="et-chart-title">
      <title id="et-chart-title">Number of people currently infected, over time</title>
      <polyline id="infected-line" class="line-infected" points="" />
    </svg>

    <p id="tick-status" aria-live="polite"></p>
    <p id="outcome-readout" class="outcome" aria-live="polite"></p>
  </section>

  <section class="explainer">
    <h2>Why there's a threshold at all</h2>
    <p>
      Every infected person recovers after a fixed number of ticks, so
      whether the disease grows or dies out depends entirely on whether each
      infected person manages to pass it on to more or less than one other
      person, on average, before they recover. That's what the slider
      controls. Below the threshold, each chain of infection is shorter than
      the last and the outbreak sputters out after a handful of cases —
      exactly what would happen if you tried to start a chain letter that
      each person only forwarded to less than one friend. Above it, each
      case produces more than one new case, and that compounding is
      explosive: in this network the flip happens narrowly, around a
      contact rate of 1.6–1.7, not at exactly 1.0, because chance and the
      shape of the network both matter at this size. Textbook epidemiology
      calls that dividing line <strong>R₀ = 1</strong>, and it's the whole
      reason public health measures target contact rate rather than trying
      to treat every case after the fact.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="explainer">
    <h2>Contact rate isn't the only lever</h2>
    <p>
      Turn the network-density slider down instead, and the outbreak can
      stay small even at a contact rate that would normally tear through
      the network — and this isn't a trick. Make the network sparse enough
      and patient zero simply isn't connected, even indirectly, to most of
      the other 119 people; whatever contact rate you dial in, an infection
      can only travel along edges that actually exist. It's the same reason
      an isolated community can go through an outbreak of the same-R₀
      disease as everywhere else and come out far less affected: not
      because contacts within it were any safer, but because the community
      is a smaller, more disconnected slice of the whole network. Masks,
      vaccination, and distancing all work by pulling the contact-rate
      slider down; closing borders, cancelling large gatherings, and
      breaking up crowds work by pulling this one down instead — two
      different levers on the same underlying mechanic.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import { initEpidemicThreshold } from "../scripts/epidemic-threshold";
  initEpidemicThreshold();
</script>

<style>
  .preset-caption {
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }

  .preset-note {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .assumption {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  .network {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 40rem;
    display: block;
    margin-inline: auto;
    background: #fafafa;
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
  }

  .network :global(.edge) {
    stroke: #d8dbe2;
    stroke-width: 1;
  }

  .network :global(.node) {
    stroke: #fff;
    stroke-width: 1;
  }

  .network :global(.node-s) {
    fill: #9ca3af;
  }

  .network :global(.node-i) {
    fill: #dc2626;
  }

  .network :global(.node-r) {
    fill: #2563eb;
  }

  .network-legend {
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }

  .swatch-s {
    background: #9ca3af;
  }

  .swatch-i {
    background: #dc2626;
  }

  .swatch-r {
    background: #2563eb;
  }

  .line-infected {
    stroke: var(--accent);
  }

  #tick-status {
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .outcome {
    font-weight: 600;
    text-align: center;
    min-height: 1.6rem;
  }
</style>
